<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bhadarwah</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bhaderwah, Bhadrawah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bhadarwah is a town in an upland valley of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doda-district">Doda district</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division of Jammu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ruled by local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs, it was for long periods a dependency of Chamba State and later came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> rulers of Jammu and Kashmir. Its people speak Bhadarwahi, and its temples of the serpent god Vasuki Nag are important local shrines.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bhadarwah.jpg" alt="Bhadarwah" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manjeet singh · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bhaderwahi_folk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bhadarwah, also spelled Bhaderwah, is a town and a tehsil in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doda-district">Doda district</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> division. The name may derive from the Sanskrit Bhadarwasa, or from Bhadarkashi, a name given to the town for the ancient temple of the goddess Bhadrakali in the area. The twelfth-century Sanskrit chronicle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajatarangini">Rajatarangini</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalhana">Kalhana</a> calls the area Bhadar Avkash, meaning a good resting place, and Bhadar Pura. Because its topography resembles the Kashmir Valley it is sometimes called Chota Kashmir, or little Kashmir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Bhaderwah was ruled by its native rulers until the eighth century. In the tenth and eleventh centuries it came under Ananta and Kalsha of neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Local hill chieftains of the Pal clan, said to be Chanderbansi Baloria Rajputs from Billawar, ruled from the eighth to the sixteenth century, beginning with Raja Radhik Pal, who is credited with building the Bhaderkali temple. According to folk history the ruler Nag Pal impressed the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> with his spiritual prowess, an event commemorated each year at the celebration called Mela Patt on Naga Panchami.</p><p>By the end of the sixteenth century Chamba had annexed it. In 1820 Raja Pahadchand fought Chamba forces under Wazir Nathu and won, but Nathu attacked again with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> help and captured Bhaderwah. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-amritsar-1846">Treaty of Amritsar</a> in 1846 the Chamba possessions west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a>, including the principality, passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulab-singh">Gulab Singh</a> of the Dogra Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. In 1886 Maharaja Pratap Singh granted it as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagir">jagir</a> to his prime minister Amar Singh, and it passed to Amar Singh's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hari-singh">Hari Singh</a> in 1912. The jagir ended in 1930, and in 1931 Bhaderwah became a tehsil of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udhampur">Udhampur</a> district.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 75,376 people in the tehsil and 11,084 in the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> formed 58.02 per cent of the tehsil, and 41.71 per cent were Muslim. Around 53 per cent gave Bhadarwahi as their mother tongue and 33.6 per cent Kashmiri. The town is known for fairs and festivals such as Mela Pat, for Kud dance and Pahari folk songs, and for heritage sites including the Ratangarh Fort, a Jamia Masjid and an ancient Vasuki Nag Temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bhaderwah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bhadrawah</b> was ruled by local Rajput chiefs; tradition dates the state to around 1400, but its early history is poorly recorded.</blockquote><a href="/state/bhadrawah-state">Bhadrawah State</a></li></ul></section>
